  "body": "Stacked on #802\n\nFork synced at v0.17.0 (branch base built before the sync; it carries #802 at c2a3f873).\n\n## What changed\n\nCamCraft now has built-in, generic workholding accessories beside #802's vises: parallels, 1-2-3 blocks, step blocks, strap (toe) clamps and soft jaws. Each is generated from a few dimensions and placed like any fixture, so the existing fixture laws judge it. There's no new collision code.\n\n- **Geometry (`app::accessory`).** Each kind is one or more closed, outward shells that touch but never overlap, so the mesh law's ray-parity inside test holds. A strap clamp's stud is drawn below the strap and above it, not through it. Holes and slots are drawn solid, and the stud is a 16-sided polygon whose flats touch its diameter, so the shells never hold less steel than the real part. Nonsense is refused by name, naming the accessory and the dimension: a non-finite, zero, negative or over-5 m dimension, a step count outside 1-50, a stud past the toe or into the heel block, or a heel block as long as the strap.\n- **Placement (`commands::InsertAccessory`).** This wraps `InsertFixture`, so the accessory lands as an ordinary model wearing `FixtureDef`, with its parameters on the marker (`FixtureDef::accessory`). You place it by pose. Parallels can instead be fitted **under the stock in a built-in vise**: the height is set so the stock's top stands a stated amount over the jaws, the pair stands on the vise floor against both jaws, and the vise drops to suit (a `MoveFixture`). All of that is one undo unit. It's refused by name if the stock already stands that high, or if two parallels don't fit in the opening.\n- **Editing (`commands::SetAccessory`).** A resize regenerates the mesh through `UpdateModel` and swaps the marker, in one undo step. A resize keeps the kind. Re-binding keeps the parameters, and a re-import drops them, as for a vise.\n- **Soft jaws** are a standalone block (width, height, thickness) placed at a jaw face, not a vise option. #802's review reshaped the vise's jaw profile (`jaw_body_depth`), and a soft-jaw option would have to reshape it again while #802 is still open. The reasoning is in the decision file.\n- **Project file.** The fixture marker gets an optional `accessory` key, tagged by `kind`, with no version bump. An older build ignores it and keeps the facets. On load, a key that breaks the law is dropped and logged, and the fixture stays as plain steel.\n- **UI.**\n  - \"Add accessory…\" on the Fixtures node's menu and the Fixtures page, sharing the fixture library's modal. You pick a kind, edit its figures, then type a pose or, for parallels, pick a vise and set \"Stock top over the jaws\".\n  - Each placed accessory's row shows its figures and \"Apply sizes\" (one `SetAccessory`). The drafts are kept per row in a map, following #802's per-vise opening drafts, so two rows never reset each other.\n- **MCP.**\n  - `add_accessory`: kind, setup, position/rotation or `under_stock_in_vise` + `above`, `steps`, and dimension overrides.\n  - `set_accessory`: dimension overrides and `steps`.\n  - Each call is one History step. No follow-up issue is needed. The `camcraft-mcp` skill's tool table is a process file, so I left it alone; it can list the tools in the next process sync.\n- **Decisions:** `docs/decisions/789-workholding-accessories.md`.\n\n## Defaults\n\nThese are our own generic figures in common inch sizes; no vendor model is copied. The code stores millimetres.\n\n| Item | Defaults |\n|---|---|\n| Parallels (a pair) | height 1 in, thickness 1/8 in, length 6 in, spacing 4 in (gap between inner faces) |\n| 1-2-3 block | 3 x 2 x 1 in, holes drawn solid |\n| Step block | 6 steps, rise 1/2 in, run 3/4 in, width 1 in |\n| Strap clamp | strap 6 x 1 1/4 x 5/8 in; stud 1/2 in, standing 1 1/2 in above the strap (nut and washer included), 2 1/4 in from the toe; heel block 1 1/4 in long; clamp height 1 in |\n| Soft jaw | width 6 in, height 2 in, thickness 3/4 in |\n| Parallels fit | stock top 1/4 in over the jaws |\n\n## How tested\n\nPinning tests:\n\n- `app builtin_accessory` (3 tests):\n  - `every_kind_builds_closed_shells_of_its_dimensions`: every kind, plus step blocks of 1-8 steps and a stud wider than the strap. Checks the mesh law's 2-cycle closure, the exact outward volume, the bounds, and that `FixtureMesh::build` accepts the mesh. The 1-2-3 block measures 1 x 2 x 3 in.\n  - `nonsense_parameters_are_refused_by_name`\n  - `placing_and_resizing_are_one_undo_each_and_survive_a_save`: placement and resize are each one undo, the revision moves, undo restores the facets, redo works, every kind's parameters survive a save/load and stay editable, and a hand-edited nonsense key loads as plain steel. A kind change and an imported fixture are refused.\n- `app accessory_collisions` (2 tests):\n  - `a_strap_clamp_stud_strikes_a_low_link_and_the_routed_link_clears_it`: the clamp is judged by its mesh. A link at z 50 across the stud is struck by the post gate's per-op law (`fixture_struck::op_hits`). The link #489 would lay (up, across at the clamp box top + 5 mm, down) is clear. The same z 50 over the strap beside the stud is clear too.\n  - `parallels_fitted_under_the_stock_lift_it_over_the_jaws`: the face pass the 6 in vise's jaws struck posts clean once the parallels are fitted. Checks the height, spacing and positions, that one undo restores the vise, and the named refusals.\n- `app mcp_accessory`: `accessories_are_placed_fitted_resized_and_undone_over_mcp`.\n- `ui` lib:\n  - `fixture_accessory_tests` (5 tests): the menu and page entry, a strap clamp placed from the dialog plus undo, parallels fitted from the dialog into the setup's vise, a refusal named in the dialog, and two rows keeping their own size drafts with Apply landing `SetAccessory` plus undo.\n  - `tests::fixture_rows::a_built_in_accessory_row_fits_the_default_pane` (structural).\n- Updated menu-label asserts in `fixture_library_tests` and `fixture_ux_tests`.\n- Mutation check: with the stud's upper shell dropped from the strap clamp mesh, the strap clamp test fails.\n\nGates:\n\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- Quick gate (`cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/issue/788-builtin-vises`): green, app (lib + every gate-mapped suite, including the three new ones) 197 s, ui lib + 11 suites 32 s, 0 failed.\n- Linux batch gate: running; its result will be added as a PR comment.\n- No snapshot PNG changed. The new \"Add accessory…\" button on the Fixtures page may move a release-lane golden that shows that page; those are regenerated at the cut.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- I haven't checked the GUI by hand: opening the dialog in the running app, placing each accessory and seeing it drawn in the viewport, fitting parallels into a vise, and editing a row's sizes.\n- #489's automatic routing over a clamp: #489 isn't on main, so the pin judges the link #489 would lay with the very law #489 consults, not the router itself. Once both are on main, a router-driven pin can be added to `fixture_route`.\n- Accessories on rotary or tilted setups.\n\nChangelog: Built-in workholding accessories in the fixture library (parallels with a fit under the stock in a built-in vise, 1-2-3 and step blocks, strap clamps, soft jaws): placed and resized in one undoable step each, judged by the fixture mesh laws (#789)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #789\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX\n\n\n",
